{"archetypes":[{"name":"shell-cleanup","focus_area":"code-robustness","weight":1,"rationale":"git-force-push.sh uses manual rm -f calls for GIT_STATUS_STDERR without a trap, creating a leak risk on unexpected exits; create-pr.sh adds the var to its trap but git-force-push.sh does not.","prompt_body":"Inspect `scripts/git-force-push.sh` for temp-file lifecycle: the new `GIT_STATUS_STDERR` variable is initialised to empty string, assigned via `mktemp`, then cleaned up with explicit `rm -f` calls on each early-exit path — but there is no `trap cleanup EXIT` equivalent to cover unexpected exits or signals. Verify that every code path from the `mktemp` call to script exit either reaches an explicit `rm -f` or is otherwise safe. Also check whether `GIT_STATUS_STDERR` declared at top scope as an empty string causes any issue when `rm -f \"$GIT_STATUS_STDERR\"` is called without the `mktemp` assignment having run (e.g. if argument parsing exits early before `mktemp`). Compare with `create-pr.sh`'s trap-based cleanup approach and assess whether the inconsistency is a risk. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"caller-compat","focus_area":"architecture","weight":1,"rationale":"git-force-push.sh now has a new exit-1 path that emits BRANCH= but no PUSHED= or STATUS= keys; existing callers (merge-pr.sh, /implement Step 8b, rebase-rebump-subprocedure.md) were written to expect PUSHED=/STATUS= on every exit-1.","prompt_body":"Trace every documented caller of `scripts/git-force-push.sh` — `scripts/create-pr.sh`, `scripts/merge-pr.sh`, `/implement` Step 8b, and `skills/implement/references/rebase-rebump-subprocedure.md` step 5 — and determine how each one parses the script's stdout keys on an exit-1 result. The new dirty-tree exit path emits `BRANCH=` but deliberately omits `PUSHED=` and `STATUS=`. Check whether any caller does unconditional key extraction that would silently produce empty or wrong values when those keys are absent, or whether any caller's error message would be misleading (e.g. reporting `STATUS=diverged_retry_failed` when the actual cause was a dirty tree). Also verify `scripts/create-pr.sh` suppresses `git-force-push.sh`'s stdout with `>/dev/null` on the escalation path so the missing keys cannot pollute its own `PR_*` output contract. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
